\title{Greedy Avoidance of Distinct-Index Pair Sums:\\Exact Classification, Range, and Algorithms}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 25, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an increasing greedy sequence beginning at $1$ which, at each stage, selects the least larger natural number not representable as the sum of values at two distinct earlier indices. Distinctness changes the behavior substantially: the opening values are $1,2,4$, after which the sequence is an arithmetic progression of common difference $3$. We prove the exact formula
\[
a_0=1,\qquad a_1=2,\qquad a_n=3n-2\quad(n\ge2),
\]
establish uniqueness, derive $a_{n+3}=a_{n+2}+3$, and classify the range as $\{2\}\cup\{3k+1:k\ge0\}$. The proof separates a local minimality certificate from a global admissibility certificate. The initial terms $1$ and $2$ forbid the two candidates immediately above each stable term, while reduction modulo $3$ proves that the next candidate cannot be a distinct-index pair sum. Exact prefix counts, density, algorithms, applications, and extensions are also developed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Greedy constructions in additive combinatorics often combine a local instruction with an expanding global constraint. Here the legal status of a candidate depends on all pairs in the preceding prefix. Begin at $1$ and choose at each stage the least larger natural number that is not the sum of values at two distinct earlier indices. Computation suggests
\[
1,2,4,7,10,13,16,19,22,25,\ldots.
\]
We prove that this pattern is forced.

Two mechanisms explain the result. If $x$ is a stable current term, then $x+1=1+x$ and $x+2=2+x$ are forbidden. On the other hand, all terms except the exceptional $2$ are congruent to $1$ modulo $3$. A sum of two distinct earlier terms is consequently congruent only to $0$ or $2$ modulo $3$, so $x+3$ is legal. Local obstruction and global coloring therefore identify the least successor exactly.

Distinctness is essential. With prefix $(1,2)$, the candidate $4$ remains legal because $4=2+2$ would reuse the same index. Thus the condition creates both the transient and the stable modulus.

\section{Definitions}
Let $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and let $a=(a_n)_{n\ge0}$ be a sequence in $\mathbb N$.

\begin{definition}[Restricted prior pair-sum set]
For $n\ge0$, define
\[
\Sigma_n(a)=\{a_i+a_j:0\le i<j<n\}.
\]
Every sum uses two distinct indices. Equal values may be used only if they occur at different positions.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissibility]
A candidate $z$ is admissible after stage $n$ if
\[
a_n<z\quad\text{and}\quad z\notin\Sigma_{n+1}(a).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Greedy successor and trajectory]
A greedy successor at stage $n$ is the least admissible natural number. A distinct-summand greedy trajectory is a sequence with $a_0=1$ whose term $a_{n+1}$ is the greedy successor at every stage $n$.
\end{definition}

The minimum always exists because the forbidden set is finite. Define the candidate sequence $c$ by
\[
c_0=1,\qquad c_1=2,\qquad c_n=3n-2\quad(n\ge2).
\]

\section{Boundary behavior and modular structure}
At stage $0$ there is no pair of distinct earlier indices, so the least number above $1$ is $2$. At stage $1$, the only restricted pair sum is $1+2=3$; hence $3$ is forbidden and $4$ is selected. The representation $4=2+2$ is unavailable because there is only one index carrying $2$.

\begin{lemma}[Residue exclusion]
If $i<j$, then $c_i+c_j\not\equiv1\pmod3$. In particular, no later candidate term, all of which are congruent to $1$ modulo $3$, is a restricted pair sum.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every term except $c_1=2$ is congruent to $1$ modulo $3$. If neither summand is $2$, their sum is congruent to $2$. If exactly one is $2$, their sum is congruent to $0$. Both cannot be the unique term $2$, since the indices are distinct. Thus residue $1$ never occurs.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Two-seed blocking]
For every $n\ge0$, after the current term $c_{n+2}=3n+4$, the candidates $c_{n+2}+1$ and $c_{n+2}+2$ are restricted pair sums from the available prefix.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The current index $n+2$ differs from the seed indices. Therefore
\[
c_{n+2}+1=c_{n+2}+c_0,
\qquad
c_{n+2}+2=c_{n+2}+c_1
\]
are sums at distinct indices.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Stable admissibility]
For every $n\ge0$, $c_{n+3}=c_{n+2}+3$ is admissible after stage $n+2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is larger than the current term and congruent to $1$ modulo $3$. The residue-exclusion lemma shows that no restricted pair sum has that residue.
\end{proof}

\section{Classification and uniqueness}
\begin{theorem}[Existence of the explicit trajectory]
The sequence
\[
c_0=1,\qquad c_1=2,\qquad c_n=3n-2\quad(n\ge2)
\]
satisfies the distinct-summand greedy rule.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first two transitions were checked directly. Thereafter stable admissibility shows that $c_{n+3}$ is legal, while two-seed blocking excludes the only two integers strictly between $c_{n+2}$ and $c_{n+3}$. Hence $c_{n+3}$ is the least legal successor.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite-history invariance]
If two sequences agree from index $0$ through index $n$, then their restricted pair-sum sets at that stage agree, and they have exactly the same greedy successor.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every restricted sum is formed from two values in the common prefix. Thus the forbidden sets and current terms coincide, so their sets of admissible candidates and their minima coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of a least admissible successor]
For any fixed history, two greedy successors must be equal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x$ and $y$ are both least admissible, minimality gives $x\le y$ and $y\le x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete classification]
A sequence $a$ satisfies the distinct-summand greedy rule if and only if
\[
a_0=1,\qquad a_1=2,\qquad a_n=3n-2\quad(n\ge2).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The reverse implication is the existence theorem. For the forward implication, use strong induction. Suppose $a_i=c_i$ through index $n$. Finite-history invariance says that $a_{n+1}$ and $c_{n+1}$ are greedy successors for the same history. Uniqueness makes them equal. The fixed initial value starts the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Stable increment]
Every trajectory satisfying the rule obeys
\[
a_{n+3}=a_{n+2}+3
\]
for all $n\ge0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitution of the classified formula gives both sides as $3n+7$.
\end{proof}

The increments are therefore $1,2,3,3,\ldots$, and strict monotonicity follows.

\section{Range, counting, and density}
\begin{theorem}[Exact range]
For every trajectory satisfying the rule,
\[
\{a_n:n\ge0\}=\{2\}\cup\{3k+1:k\ge0\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The terms are $a_0=1=3\cdot0+1$, $a_1=2$, and $a_n=3(n-1)+1$ for $n\ge2$. Conversely, $2=a_1$, $3\cdot0+1=a_0$, and $3k+1=a_{k+1}$ for $k\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exact cutoff count]
Let
\[
A(N)=\#\bigl(\{a_n:n\ge0\}\cap\{0,1,\ldots,N\}\bigr).
\]
Then $A(0)=0$, and for $N\ge1$,
\[
A(N)=\left\lfloor\frac{N-1}{3}\right\rfloor+1+\mathbf 1_{N\ge2}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The integers $3k+1\le N$ correspond to $0\le k\le(N-1)/3$, giving the floor term plus one. The exceptional value $2$ contributes exactly when $N\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Natural density]
The value set has natural density $1/3$:
\[
\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{A(N)}{N+1}=\frac13.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The floor differs from its argument by less than $1$, and the exceptional contribution is bounded. After division by $N+1$, the bounded errors vanish.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
\begin{algorithm}[Direct restricted-sumset generation]
To generate $m$ terms, initialize the list with $1$. At each stage form all sums at pairs of distinct indices, scan upward from one more than the current term, and append the first candidate absent from the forbidden set.
\end{algorithm}

A straightforward rebuilding implementation performs $O(m^3)$ arithmetic operations and uses $O(m^2)$ memory at the largest stage. Maintaining the sumset incrementally reduces total inserted sums to $O(m^2)$. This algorithm applies directly from the definition and does not presuppose the classification.

\begin{algorithm}[Closed-form generation]
For each $0\le n<m$, output $1$ when $n=0$, $2$ when $n=1$, and $3n-2$ otherwise.
\end{algorithm}

Materializing $m$ terms takes $O(m)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space beyond the output. A random-access term query is constant-time. Range membership is also constant-time: $x$ occurs exactly when $x=2$ or $x\equiv1\pmod3$. Its index is $1$ for $x=2$, $0$ for $x=1$, and $(x+2)/3$ for $x\ge4$ in residue class $1$.

\section{Numerical demonstrations}
Direct and closed-form generation both give
\[
1,2,4,7,10,13,16,19,22,25.
\]
For the prefix $(1,2,4,7)$, the restricted pair sums are
\[
3,5,6,8,9,11.
\]
Thus $8=1+7$ and $9=2+7$ are forbidden, while $10$ is absent and selected.

At cutoff $N=100$, there are $34$ values of the form $3k+1$ and the exceptional value $2$, so $A(100)=35$ and
\[
\frac{A(100)}{101}=\frac{35}{101}\approx0.3465.
\]
The ratio approaches $1/3$ as the cutoff grows.

\section{Interpretation and applications}
The proof illustrates a reusable architecture. A \emph{local minimality certificate} excludes candidates between the current value and a proposed successor; a \emph{global admissibility certificate} proves that the proposed successor avoids all obstructions generated by the history. Here the seeds $1$ and $2$ provide the local certificate, and residue classes modulo $3$ provide the global one.

In additive combinatorics, the attained set $\{2\}\cup(1+3\mathbb N)$ has a restricted two-fold sumset avoiding residue $1$. In scheduling or resource allocation, values may represent labels while pair sums model collisions; modular classes then provide protected channels. In coding models, additive interference from two distinct previous symbols can similarly be separated from valid symbols by a congruence class. Although the rule remembers the entire prefix, its stable behavior is certified by the current value, two seeds, and one residue.

\section{Future work}
An exact union-of-intervals description of finite restricted pair-sum sets would sharpen the combinatorial picture and yield closed cardinality formulas. For sums of exactly $r\ge2$ earlier values at pairwise distinct indices, one may ask whether a finite transient is followed by an arithmetic progression and how its modulus depends on $r$.

Alternative positive seeds may exhibit phase transitions in their stable moduli and transient lengths. A general analytic bridge from eventual residue-class rigidity to normalized counting limits would systematize density arguments. Finally, bounded-error greediness---choosing any admissible value at most $E$ above the least one---raises questions about sharp density bounds and proximity to finite unions of arithmetic progressions.

\section{Conclusion}
The distinct-index pair-sum avoidance rule has the unique trajectory
\[
1,2,4,7,10,13,\ldots.
\]
After two transient increments, every step has size $3$. The two intervening candidates are blocked locally by the seeds, while the next candidate is protected globally by residue $1$ modulo $3$. This alignment yields the exact formula, exact range, finite counts, and density $1/3$, and it provides a model strategy for other history-dependent greedy avoidance processes.

\end{document}

